WEBVTT
TITLE First-Order Resolution and Unification: Terms as Trees, the Most General Unifier, and Skolemisation — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A clause is a ground clause: every variable has already been replaced by an object.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
One way out is to instantiate: write one clause per object, then resolve propositionally.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The other way out is to ask when two atoms can be made identical by one substitution.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Robinson 1965 combined the substitution and the truth-functional step into one rule.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
A term is a constant, a variable, or a function symbol applied to terms.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
So a term is a finite tree, and the symbol at the root is what the term is.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Because f may be applied to its own output, the set of terms is infinite.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
A substitution maps variables to terms, and never touches constants.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Applying {x/a, y/b} to f(x, g(y)) gives f(a, g(b)).

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Composing two substitutions means: apply the first, then the second.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The two orders can disagree, and here is a term where they do.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Associativity is what lets the algorithm keep composing without bookkeeping.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Compare the two roots first: both are f, so walk into the arguments together.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
First argument: x against a. That is a variable, so bind x to a.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Second argument: g(y) against g(b). Same symbol, so recurse: bind y to b.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Nothing is left to compare, so {x/a, y/b} is a unifier.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A clash: two different constants, or two different function symbols, at the same place.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
An occurs check: a variable against a term that contains it.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
A binding can also be undone by the next one: f(x,x) against f(a,b).

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Two of the three are fatal at once; the third only fails later.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Many substitutions can unify a pair; one of them is the weakest.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Most general means every other unifier is this one followed by something.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The most general unifier is unique up to renaming of variables.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Matching is the one-way case: the pattern has variables, the other side does not.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
First put the quantifiers in front, then remove the existentials one at a time.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
An existential with no forall to its left becomes a constant.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
An existential under one forall becomes a function of that variable.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Satisfiability is preserved; logical equivalence is not.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Rename the two clauses apart before doing anything else.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Pick one positive and one negative atom with the same predicate, and unify them.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Apply that unifier to everything else and keep it: that is the resolvent.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Factoring handles two equal atoms inside one clause, and it strengthens the clause.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Three clauses: every man is mortal, Socrates is a man, Socrates is not mortal.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Unify Man(x) with Man(s): x becomes s, and the resolvent is Mortal(s).

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
The lifting lemma: every ground step is an instance of a general step.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Soundness is one case; completeness is Herbrand plus lifting.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
First-order validity is only semi-decidable: a refutation is a proof, a failure is not.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Equality is not built in: without extra rules, a = a cannot be derived.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
GATE CSE 2017 Set 1: from for all x there exists y, only two of the four follow.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Four questions decide every case: clauses, unifier, arity, and what is preserved.

